#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074605} :activity/id #uuid "84bb85b0-fcbc-5f41-956b-e9fb4753720a" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048460 :topic/id #uuid "a1872f62-c9fe-5c68-8bb3-3c9dac0602b3" :topic/math-academy-id 528} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074605 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186075052 :tutorial/content "Remember that a *unit* fraction is a fraction containing ${\\color{blue}{1}}$ in the numerator. So, for example, the fractions\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{blue}{1}}2, \\qquad\\dfrac{\\color{blue}{1}}{12}\n$$\n\nare both unit fractions, whereas\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{red}{2}}9, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\color{red}{3}}{5}\n$$\n\nare *not* unit fractions\n\nIn this lesson, we will use models to divide unit fractions by whole numbers.\n\nLet's use a fraction model to find the value of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2} \\div 2\n$$\n\nWe start with a square model of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac12\n$$\n\nsplit *vertically*.\n\n![](images/cc/ccfe0f457342c456200c98cfc46e63bdc1153698282085164eeafae5c2092c45.png)\n\nTo divide\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nby ${\\color{blue}2}$, we split the above whole into $\\color{blue}2$ equal parts *horizontally*. Then, we remove any shaded parts that are not in the bottom row.\n\n![](images/79/7940073ba05d3136ee1d465530a60bc3624b7aa06fd7484d30797758a28976d7.png)\n\nFrom the picture on the right, we obtain that the shaded part represents\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nof the whole.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2} \\div 2 = \\dfrac{1}{4}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "5efb47fa-47c0-50c8-9c53-0a64bfc732ea" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8924 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "f5584f93-514d-5a2f-bcbb-77e4a6db1fab" :step/math-academy-id 170369 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074606}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074606 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073716 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073486 :question/id #uuid "4fae5cf1-65c9-5217-89ec-54b40e80f293" :question/math-academy-id "e-14418"} :know